From 65c907e8dd22f4cba9ae340faf2c3f6469476c57 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: turbopack-wasi build <build@turbopack-wasi.invalid>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 12:42:22 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 19/20] turbopack-ecmascript-plugins, next-napi-bindings: drop
 the wasmtime SWC plugin backend on wasm

SWC transform plugins are themselves WebAssembly modules, executed by an
embedded wasm runtime (swc_plugin_backend_wasmtime -> wasmtime). That backend
can't run inside a wasm guest, and it pulls in the cap-std / cap-primitives /
wasi-common stack, which no longer builds for wasm32-wasip1-threads (recent std
dropped the wasi_ext OpenOptionsExt/MetadataExt methods those crates rely on).

`swc_plugin_backend_wasmtime` is the sole path to that whole stack, so gate the
dependency behind `cfg(not(target_family = "wasm"))` and swap the concrete
`WasmtimeRuntime` for a small `NoopRuntime` stub that implements
`swc_plugin_runner::runtime::Runtime`. The plugin *host* types (from swc_core's
__plugin_transform_host feature, pure Rust) stay, so the transform pipeline
type-checks unchanged. Constructing/cloning plugin modules still works; an app
with no configured SWC plugins never touches the runtime, and actually invoking
one returns a clear error instead of silently doing nothing.
